From b2a8fd21b26fc5d06910a0e7562b805571ffd48d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: colum <git@volf.co>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 21:20:40 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] dsv41: TF_DS_EXACT_RESUME (off by default) keeps a prompt
 pass's own end as an off-grid state with the window's decoder-input rows, so
 the next turn resumes at the exact token; TF_DS_DEBUG_SPLIT=p cuts a cold
 pass's chunk at p for step 0
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A kept prompt state exists today only at a 2048-row chunk end at or before the prompt's replay (lanes.zig's keep
hook and snapSaveFn, usable's grid rule), so a turn recomputes its own new text, the previous reply, the 128-row
window a resume replays and up to 2,047 rows of grid slack. Baseten's engine resumes at the exact token: the
encoder runs over the new rows and the decoder is replayed over the last 128 rows it kept plus those rows. This
brings that to this engine for the state a prompt pass computes (phase A: no decode-written rows).

TF_DS_EXACT_RESUME (`1` only; unset, `0`, `off` or anything else leave it off, as the series' switches do) and
`exact_resume=1;` in the ranks' feature string, so a pair that disagrees refuses at the hello. With it on:
- replayCut copies the chunk's decoder-input rows (h bf16 and pre fp32) of the pass's last `window` positions into
  the slot's 128-row tail, a ring by position, appending across chunks (Model.tails; 5.2 MB a slot and rank).
- after the last chunk and before the first round the pass keeps its own end (the prompt's length rounded down to
  even) as a state carrying that tail (Snap.tail, tail_start/tail_end); the prompt cache is told it exists (Rules.exact
  adds the end mark to Store.marks and lets a lookup past the tail/window rule, the family's usable still deciding).
- usable and snapSaveFn accept an off-grid `at` only when it carries a tail whose rows hold the new replay
  (tail_start <= len - window, tail_end >= at); snapBytesFn reports the larger state, and the tail is saved and
  restored inside the existing snap_save/snap_restore handling on both ranks (no new frame kind, no field added).
- a resumed pass runs its first chunk only to the next 2048-row end, so every later chunk end lands on the grid and
  the keep hook, shared cuts, edit marks and spill keeps keep firing; replayCut prepends the tail's rows
  [replay, start) to the chunk's h and pre and to its positions, so the decoder runs on [replay, chunk end) as a cold
  pass would, with rs.floor = replay as today; the new tail is stashed for the next turn.

Grid keeps are exactly as they were (the hook, last_end, snapSaveFn's rule); the end state is one extra Snap a
conversation and supersedes the conversation's previous end state. With the switch off no tail is allocated, the
chunk loop's rows and every frame's bytes are what they were, and the feature string is unchanged.

Phase A resumes at even positions only (a ratio-2 layer refuses an odd chunk start; the end is rounded down to even)
and keeps off-grid states in memory only: with the switch on, the spill tier (its block store is 2048-token blocks),
TF_DS_KV_SHARD=1, TF_DS_PREFILL_SLICE and image prompts are refused at start (error.ExactResumeUnsupported, named in
native.explain). Phase B (keeping the reply's decode-written rows, LOSSY by construction) is not built, and the
odd-start case is not ported.

TF_DS_DEBUG_SPLIT=p (step 0's identity experiment, even p) cuts the chunk holding p in a cold pass in two at p
([grid, p) then [p, next grid)), every other chunk as today: the shape an off-grid resume produces, with no Snap
changes, so `--logits-sha 1` compares a split pass's prompt logits with an unsplit one's (it already printed them).

Tests: zig build test passes, including the new host tests: a tail's prepend and rows (replayCut's `pre`, a tail
short of the window refused); a pass's tail across chunks (prompts under the window, an exact grid multiple, a last
window spanning two chunks, a resumed pass's re-stash), a turn's deltas of 1/127/128/129/2048/2049 tokens, and the
prepend's rows in the position order a cold pass lays out; usable/snapSaveFn's acceptance (an off-grid state with a
covering tail resumes, a tail that does not hold the replay is refused, a grid state as ever, the pass's end kept
with its tail and refused without the switch); the chunk loop's rows (as ever from a chunk end, the next one from an
off-grid resume); the feature string with the switch on and off; Model.open's init list for the new fields (0114's
bug class); and the switch's parse. tools/resume_exact.py (Sparks repo) runs the pair gate: a conversation of turns
appending 1..5,000 tokens, each turn's cold reply hash against its warm one, and cached/prefill/TTFT with the switch
off and on. Not run on hardware: step 0's split-pass logits comparison and the cold-equals-warm reply gate are the
first live measurements (docs/baseten-ultrafast-plan.md item 3).

    The cache's gate: `liveWhy` skips the native grid rule for a state whose `at` is off the grid when the store's
    `Rules.exact` is set, since the family's `usable` above it is that state's own gate — without it a store built with
    a grid (the offline `tf-dsv41-lanes` binary, which sets one) kept the end state and never found it. Test: an
    off-grid end state is found on a grid store with the switch on and passed over without it.
